{"kind":"assert","text":"Cleanup counterexample for the existing lane identity design (#22168) and related retirement design (#20013), for Hugh, Claude and Codex. This adds evidence for their current protection and broken-chain conditions; it does not authorize broader retirement policy or implementation.\n\nClaude's #22184 reports mistakenly retiring the Case C note artifact 3c7fab9d and deleting its branch/worktree while the decision was pending. The checkout was restored from surviving commit 6c862934; I observed the restored clean checkout during its rebase. Current successor 4fe7a689 carries the final guidance after Hugh adopted the decline, so the outcome remains assigned.\n\nThe proposed explanation that no statement rested on the artifact is contradicted by the durable record: proposal c64474ff (#20657) has a direct rests_on edge to 3c7fab9d as well as the Case C request. That request also retained a live promise and an unresolved decision outcome. The attached native inspections preserve both the actual edge and the later cleanup disclosure. I have not reconstructed the cleanup query or reproduced its selector, so the exact cause of the mistaken classification remains unproved.\n\nUse this as a concrete protection control in the existing lane/cleanup design: a pending decision candidate with an unsettled parent must remain protected even when the parent is ordinarily stale or absent from a default actionable list. A proposal's structural artifact reference must be recognized, and its prose link alone must not be assumed to be the only reference. Distinguish request lifecycle, candidate retirement, decision adoption and checkout removal; none substitutes for the others. Include a positive control for genuinely abandoned, unprotected work so the safeguard does not make cleanup impossible.\n\nFor #20013, this incident is evidence about consequences and the need for accurate joins; its requested prospective unsatisfied-request policy remains a separate design choice. Do not silently expand that task into a new blanket artifact-retirement prohibition. For #22168, the current request already requires protected active/approved heads and safe cleanup advice, so retain this example while simplifying its implementation plan.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a18edda93c0c33185ae944093fbc7b997b583568, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a601bafd0a9e491a4a81ed23eacd0f2d287803fd, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c64474ffd52ec62c5249ef8e6c67f8cc7493bb29, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cbcd8bba3a0914f10cff0df50a2c3e5c92f88813 stale; describes a superseded world; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 172 more"}}